<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Ksani</h1><p class="st-meta">1380 CE – 1777 CE (approx.) · capital Akhalgori</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Georgian highland duchy in the Ksani valley whose hereditary eristavis guarded the routes north of Kartli for four centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Ksani valley north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> was organised as a saeristavo, or duchy, of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a>, and from the late 14th century the office became hereditary in a local family known as the Ksani eristavis. After 1490 they served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli">Kingdom of Kartli</a>, with their seat at Akhalgori.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes ruled mountain communities of farmers and herders, raising troops for the kings and guarding passes towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetia">Ossetia</a>. They often acted independently, intriguing with Persian governors and rival nobles. Two of their dukes, Shalva and Elizbar, rose with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidzina-cholokashvili">Bidzina Cholokashvili</a> against Persian rule in 1659 and were executed by the shah, and the Georgian church venerates them as martyrs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After repeated disloyalty, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a> abolished the duchy in 1777 and incorporated its lands into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli-kakheti">Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti</a>. The family palace at Akhalgori survives, and the valley later became part of the Russian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> after 1801.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-ksani.jpg" alt="Duchy of Ksani" width="320" height="428"><figcaption>Image: Ercwlff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Duchy_of_Ksani_17-18_centuries_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1380 CE – 1777 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Akhalgori</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shalva, Elizbar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ksani, Saeristavo of Ksani</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1578&amp;focus=duchy-of-ksani" aria-label="Duchy of Ksani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5760 679 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Ksani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6080 935L6036 930M6080 935L6091 947M6080 935L6091 947"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6036 930h0M6091 947h0M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6080 935h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6080 935h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1578&amp;focus=duchy-of-ksani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
